Author:     totalwindupflightsystems <totalwindupflightsystems@gmail.com>
AuthorDate: Fri Sep 18 09:17:25 2026 -0500
Commit:     totalwindupflightsystems <totalwindupflightsystems@gmail.com>
CommitDate: Fri Sep 18 09:17:25 2026 -0500

    fix: preserve shell operators when rebuilding an aggregate sandbox MODIFY
    
    The aggregate MODIFY path rebuilt the command with " ".join(segments), which
    DROPPED every shell operator between segments: `go test ./... | tee /tmp/log`
    came back as `unshare ... bash -c 'go test ./...' tee /tmp/log`. The standalone
    wrapper executes the `modified` string verbatim, so a modify verdict silently
    ran a different command — the trailing stage never ran as a stage and its words
    became arguments (bash's $0/$@) of the sandboxed first command.
    
    Rebuild from the parser's own structure instead:
    
    * parser.py records a source span per part (`_split_parts`) and exposes
      `segment_texts`, `operator_sequence`, `rebuild_command` and
      `structure_preserved`. A replaced segment is swapped on its exact span, so
      operators, redirections, quoting and whitespace elsewhere are copied verbatim
      from the original text.
    * decider.py replaces each rewritten segment in place and refuses any rebuild
      that would change the structure: the segments handed in must be the ones the
      original parses to, and the rebuilt string must keep the same segment count
      and operator sequence. When either guard fails the verdict degrades to a
      whole-command wrap (the original command as ONE quoted argument) instead of
      shipping a restructured command — the sandbox still applies, and no operator
      can be dropped or re-bound.
    * the aggregate MODIFY now carries the rule id that rewrote the first modified
      segment (segment order; the built-in auto-sandbox layer for sandbox verdicts).
      Previously rule_id was dropped, so callers had to replay the sandbox layer
      just to learn provenance.
    
    Tests: pinned the board's vectors (`go test ./... | tee f`, `npm test | tail
    -5`, a three-stage pipe, `go test 2>&1 | tee f`, script + stderr redirect) with
    exact rebuilt strings, segment counts and operator sequences; multi-stage and
    non-first-stage wraps; the canonical "tee must stay a stage" case; the pre-fix
    join rejected by the structure guard; misaligned segments and escaping hazards
    degrading to the whole-command wrap; `segment_texts` pinned against
    `parse_command`. Stale comments that documented the missing rule_id and the
    dropped operator as pre-existing behaviour were corrected in
    test_interruptor.py, test_escape_waves.py and test_interruptor_integration.py,
    plus a bridge-level regression (the level whose output the wrapper executes).
    `_first_sandbox_rule` is kept only as an independent cross-check of the
    provenance now reported on the result.
    
    Verification: suite 567 -> 588 passed / 5 skipped (CI selection: 586 passed);
    ruff clean; scripts/board_id_guard.py OK; bash -n clean on the CLI + scripts.
    Pre-fix RED by reverting decider.py alone: 29 new/updated tests fail with the
    dropped-operator strings (`... 'go test ./...' tee /tmp/log`); reverting
    parser.py makes the helper imports a collection error. Live semantic probe with
    a stubbed `unshare` on PATH: the rebuilt pipeline writes the tee output, the
    pre-fix string never runs the trailing stage (rc 0, no file).
    
    Addresses TJ-GAP-066.
    
    Co-authored-by: Alexis Okuwa <wojonstech@gmail.com>
